  "grammar": {
    "title": "The Verb To Be (am, is, are)",
    "explanation": "We use **am**, **is**, and **are** to talk about people and things.\n\nUse **am** with I. Use **is** with one person or one thing. Use **are** with you or many things.\n\nWe use **am**, **is**, and **are** to say what something is like. We put an adjective after the verb.\n\nWe also use **am**, **is**, and **are** to say where something is. We put a place word after the verb.",
    "examples": [
      "*I *am* happy.* (I + am)",
      "*My shirt *is* blue.* (one thing + is)",
      "*My pants *are* black.* (many things + are)",
      "*Her dress *is* red.* (one thing + is)",
      "*His shoes *are* new.* (many things + are)",
      "*The hat *is* on the table.* (place)"
    ],
    "practice": [
      {
        "q": "I ___ happy. (am / is)",
        "a": "I am happy."
      },
      {
        "q": "My shirt ___ blue. (is / are)",
